;; Conformance fixture: dispatch-opt/retired-draft-name
;;
;; rf2-8rtuiq (Mike ruled OPTION A — the shipped-names-only tombstone rule):
;; a dedicated retired-name error id may exist ONLY for names that shipped in a
;; released artefact. The dispatch opts `:rf.world/inputs` (renamed to `:rf.cofx`,
;; EP-0017) and `:dispatched-at` (retired, EP-0010) only ever named a fact in the
;; spec's own DRAFTS — they never shipped — so they earn NO dedicated retired-name
;; error id. They ride the GENERIC unrecognised-opt surface
;; (`:rf.warning/unknown-dispatch-opt`, per Conventions §The tombstone rule):
;;   - the key is UNRECOGNISED, so the no-silent-swallow warning fires;
;;   - the dispatch PROCEEDS unchanged (the warning is observational, not refusal);
;;   - the retired key is NOT aliased to `:rf.cofx` and does NOT become a
;;     recordable coeffect;
;;   - the warning message appends a did-you-mean naming the canonical
;;     replacement.
;;
;; This fixture supersedes the old `cofx-world-inputs-retired.edn` (which pinned a
;; dedicated `:rf.error/world-inputs-renamed` hard error, now deleted).
;;
;; The load-bearing behavioural contract asserted via `:final-app-db` (portable,
;; not dev-gated): the handler RUNS even when the retired draft opt is supplied
;; (no throw, no refusal), and the retired key does not leak into the recordable
;; coeffects. The `:trace-emissions` leg additionally pins the generic warning +
;; the did-you-mean hint (dev-trace surface; the harness runs dev-enabled, as the
;; other trace-emission fixtures do).
;;
;; A port that THROWS on the retired draft key (a dedicated hard error), or that
;; SILENTLY ALIASES it to `:rf.cofx`, FAILS: the handler would not have run
;; cleanly, or the retired fact would have leaked in.

{:fixture/id           :dispatch-opt/retired-draft-name
 :fixture/spec-version "1.0"
 :fixture/capabilities #{:core/event-handler}
 :fixture/doc          "rf2-8rtuiq / Conventions §The tombstone rule: the retired DRAFT dispatch opts `:rf.world/inputs` / `:dispatched-at` earn no dedicated error id — they ride the generic `:rf.warning/unknown-dispatch-opt` surface with a did-you-mean, the dispatch PROCEEDS unchanged, and the retired key is NOT aliased. A port that throws a dedicated error or silently aliases the key FAILS this fixture."

 :fixture/registry
 {:event {:probe/retired-draft {:doc "A plain handler; the load-bearing contract is that it RUNS despite the retired draft opt, not that the dispatch is refused."}}}

 :fixture/handlers
 {:event {:probe/retired-draft [[:set [:ran] true]]}}

 :fixture/frame-config {}

 :fixture/dispatches
 [;; Retired DRAFT opt — an unrecognised opt: the dispatch PROCEEDS (the handler
  ;; runs, setting :ran true), the key is NOT aliased to :rf.cofx.
  {:event [:probe/retired-draft] :rf.world/inputs {:rf/time-ms 1}}
  ;; Control — the canonical replacement opt is accepted; the handler runs.
  {:event [:probe/retired-draft] :rf.cofx {:rf/time-ms 1781078400123}}]

 :fixture/expect
 {;; Both dispatches ran the handler — the retired-draft-opt dispatch was NOT
  ;; refused, and the control's canonical :rf.cofx replacement is accepted.
  :final-app-db
  {:ran true}

  ;; The retired draft key rides the GENERIC unknown-dispatch-opt warning
  ;; (partial-match: absent keys ignored). A port emitting this warning for the
  ;; retired key proves it treats it as a plain unrecognised opt, not a special
  ;; hard error.
  :trace-emissions
  [{:operation :rf.warning/unknown-dispatch-opt}]}}
